    "gold_solution": "```python\nimport math\nfrom gurobipy import *\n\ndef solve_problem():\n    m = Model(\"Power_Transmission_Network\")\n    m.setParam(\"TimeLimit\", 100)\n\n    # Problem Data\n    n_nodes = 5\n    nodes = list(range(n_nodes))\n    n_generators = 2\n\n    # Node coordinates (x, y) in km\n    node_coords = [(85, 6), (64, 9), (63, 55), (65, 60), (61, 78)]\n\n    # Node types\n    node_types = ['generator', 'generator', 'consumer', 'consumer', 'consumer']\n\n    # Power demands and generator capacities (MW)\n    power_demands = [0.0, 0.0, 32.0, 21.0, 17.0]\n    generator_capacities = [81.0, 72.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0]\n\n    # Wire physical properties (INDUCED PARAMETERS)\n    resistivity = 1.68e-08  # Ohm-meter\n    wire_area_mm2 = 630  # mm^2\n    wire_area_m2 = wire_area_mm2 * 1e-6  # Convert to m^2\n\n    # Operating parameters\n    operating_voltage = 345  # kV\n    line_capacity = 200  # MW\n    build_cost_per_km = 144319  # $/km\n    loss_cost_rate = 65  # $/MWh equivalent\n\n    # COMPUTE distance and resistance matrices (INDUCED COSTS)\n    # Using Ohm's Law: R = resistivity * length / area\n    def compute_distance_km(coord1, coord2):\n        return math.sqrt((coord1[0] - coord2[0])**2 + (coord1[1] - coord2[1])**2)\n\n    def compute_resistance(dist_km):\n        dist_m = dist_km * 1000  # Convert km to m\n        return resistivity * dist_m / wire_area_m2\n\n    distances = {}  # km\n    resistances = {}  # Ohm\n    for i in nodes:\n        for j in nodes:\n            if i != j:\n                dist = compute_distance_km(node_coords[i], node_coords[j])\n                distances[(i, j)] = dist\n                resistances[(i, j)] = compute_resistance(dist)\n\n    # Create edge list (undirected, i < j)\n    edges = [(i, j) for i in nodes for j in nodes if i < j]\n\n    # Decision Variables\n    # Build_i_j: Binary - whether to build line between nodes i and j\n    build = {}\n    for (i, j) in edges:\n        build[(i, j)] = m.addVar(vtype=GRB.BINARY, name=f\"Build_{i}_{j}\")\n\n    # Flow_i_j: Continuous - power flow from node i to j (MW)\n    flow = {}\n    for i in nodes:\n        for j in nodes:\n            if i != j:\n                flow[(i, j)] = m.addVar(lb=0.0, vtype=GRB.CONTINUOUS, name=f\"Flow_{i}_{j}\")\n\n    # Helper function to get build variable (handles i > j case)\n    def get_build(i, j):\n        if i < j:\n            return build[(i, j)]\n        else:\n            return build[(j, i)]\n\n    # Objective: Minimize construction cost + operational transmission cost\n    # Cost factor: converts resistance to cost coefficient\n    # Cost = LossRate * (R / V^2) * P^2\n    # factor = LossRate / V^2\n    loss_factor = loss_cost_rate / (operating_voltage ** 2)\n\n    # Construction costs (based on distance)\n    obj = quicksum(distances[(i, j)] * build_cost_per_km * build[(i, j)] for (i, j) in edges)\n\n    # Operational costs: Quadratic (I^2*R losses)\n    # Note: Gurobi supports quadratic objectives directly\n    obj += quicksum(\n        resistances[(i, j)] * loss_factor * flow[(i, j)] * flow[(i, j)]\n        for i in nodes for j in nodes if i != j\n    )\n    m.setObjective(obj, GRB.MINIMIZE)\n\n    # Flow conservation constraints\n    for node in nodes:\n        inflow = quicksum(flow[(other, node)] for other in nodes if other != node)\n        outflow = quicksum(flow[(node, other)] for other in nodes if other != node)\n        \n        if node_types[node] == 'generator':\n            # Generator: net outflow (generation) <= capacity\n            # Rewritten: inflow - outflow >= -capacity\n            m.addConstr(\n                inflow - outflow >= -generator_capacities[node],\n                name=f\"Gen_Cap_{node}\"\n            )\n        else:\n            # Consumer: net inflow >= demand\n            m.addConstr(\n                inflow - outflow >= power_demands[node],\n                name=f\"Demand_{node}\"\n            )\n\n    # Capacity constraints: Flow <= line_capacity * Build\n    for i in nodes:\n        for j in nodes:\n            if i != j:\n                m.addConstr(\n                    flow[(i, j)] <= line_capacity * get_build(i, j),\n                    name=f\"Cap_{i}_{j}\"\n                )\n\n    m.optimize()\n    return m\n```",
    "LLM_description": "Subject: Planning memo \u2014 Cost-minimizing transmission network for two generators and three consumers\n\nWe are planning a five-node transmission network that connects two generator sites to three consumer areas. The nodes are geographically dispersed, and their map positions are provided in Node Coordinates (km):\n  - N0 (generator): (85, 6)\n  - N1 (generator): (64, 9)\n  - N2 (consumer): (63, 55)\n  - N3 (consumer): (65, 60)\n  - N4 (consumer): (61, 78). The role of each node (generator vs. consumer) is identified in ['generator', 'generator', 'consumer', 'consumer', 'consumer'], with consumer requirements summarized in Consumer demands: N2: 32.0 MW, N3: 21.0 MW, N4: 17.0 MW and generator output limits specified in Generator capacities: N0: 81.0 MW, N1: 72.0 MW.\n\nThe central design decision is which transmission corridors to build between these nodes. Power can only flow along lines we elect to construct, and the capital expense for each line scales with route length at $144,319/km. Once built, each corridor must respect its maximum power transfer rating given by 200 MW. Our network must deliver enough power to each consumer to meet their demand while remaining within generator limits and line ratings.\n\nOperationally, the cost of moving power is driven by resistive heating in the conductors, which follows a square law. Losses grow with the square of current (I^2R), and because current is proportional to power divided by voltage, the physics translates to a quadratic relationship in power flow: Loss ~ Resistance \u00d7 (Power/Voltage)^2. Doubling the power flow on a given line leads to four times the resistive losses. We will operate the network at 345 kV; using higher voltage is essential here, as it reduces current and cuts heating costs with a 1/V^2 effect.\n\nOur lines will use copper conductors. The wire material specification is provided in 1.68e-08 \u03a9\u00b7m (copper), and the selected conductor size is defined in 630 mm\u00b2. Practically, wire resistance increases linearly with span length and decreases with larger cross-sectional area. This means longer routes and thinner conductors yield higher resistance and, in turn, higher quadratic losses for the same power flow. The operational penalty we assign to these resistive losses is valued at $65/MWh.\n\nA key modeling note: this is a planning framework, not a detailed real-time power-flow simulation. We treat losses as a separate financial penalty rather than physically subtracting them from nodal balances. In other words, power flow is conserved at each node; generators supply and consumers receive exactly what the plan calls for, and we account for the cost of losses externally. This approach keeps the mass balance clean while still honoring the physics that makes losses quadratic, not linear. As a consequence, we cannot simply sum flows and apply a linear cost; the square law must be explicitly reflected so that high-flow corridors are appropriately penalized.\n\nBringing these elements together, the problem is to choose which lines to build and how to route power so that all consumer demands are met, generator limits are respected, line capacities are not exceeded, and the total cost is minimized. Total cost combines:\n- Construction cost, driven by inter-nodal distance and priced per kilometer at $144,319/km.\n- Operating cost from resistive heating, which grows with the square of power flow and is valued using $65/MWh, with wire resistance set by copper\u2019s properties in 1.68e-08 \u03a9\u00b7m (copper) and the conductor size in 630 mm\u00b2, and moderated by the chosen 345 kV.\n\nIn practical terms, we are trading off topology and distance (capital) against flow concentration (quadratic heating costs). Shorter lines reduce build spend and resistance, but may push more power through fewer corridors, amplifying losses. Additional lines can relieve flows and cut heating costs, but raise construction expense. The optimal plan will find the balance between these effects to deliver all loads at minimum combined construction and operational cost.",
    "LLM_description_template": "Memo: Transmission network design with quadratic loss cost\n\nWe are planning a five\u2011node transmission network that connects two generator nodes to three consumer nodes. The locations of all nodes are given in map coordinates and will determine the distances between potential corridors; the site layout is provided in {NODE_COORDINATES}. Each node\u2019s role in the system (generator or consumer) is defined in {NODE_TYPES}, and the required load to be served at the consumer nodes is specified in {POWER_DEMANDS}. Generator output limits are constrained by {GENERATOR_CAPACITIES}.\n\nOur core decision is which transmission lines to build between these nodes and how to route power flows once the lines exist. Power can only flow on lines that we choose to construct, and each built line must respect the maximum power rating in {LINE_CAPACITY}. Construction expenditures scale with route length; the applicable cost per kilometer is {BUILD_COST_PER_KM}.\n\nOperationally, we must account for resistive heating losses on each energized line. We are using copper conductors; the wire material specification is provided in {WIRE_RESISTIVITY}, and the conductor thickness is set by {WIRE_CROSS_SECTION}. Line resistance increases with length and decreases with larger cross\u2011section, so longer, thinner lines are more lossy than shorter, thicker ones. Losses rise steeply with loading: they follow a square law with the power flow. In practical terms, if we double the power pushed through a line, the resistive losses quadruple. Because current is power divided by voltage, the heating loss on a given line scales with the line\u2019s resistance times the square of power divided by the square of the operating voltage. A higher voltage substantially reduces current and therefore these losses; our system voltage level is defined in {OPERATING_VOLTAGE}.\n\nIn this planning model, losses are treated as a cost, not as a physical subtraction from delivered energy. Nodal balances are enforced without deducting losses: power is conserved at each node, and all consumer demands must be met exactly. We then apply a financial penalty for heating, line by line, based on the physics above. The economic value of losses is charged at {LOSS_COST_RATE}, so the operational cost on a line is that rate multiplied by the resistive loss determined by its resistance and the square of its power flow relative to the voltage. This preserves the key physics: higher flows drive disproportionately higher heating costs, and higher voltage mitigates those costs via the 1/V\u00b2 effect.\n\nThe objective is to minimize the total cost of the network: the capital cost of building the selected lines plus the operating cost of resistive losses. The trade\u2011offs are intuitive:\n- Longer routes cost more to build and have higher resistance, increasing loss cost.\n- Concentrating too much flow on a single corridor looks cheap in distance terms but can be expensive due to the square law on losses.\n- Using higher voltage helps by reducing current and the associated I\u00b2R losses.\n- Flows must remain within {LINE_CAPACITY}, generators cannot exceed {GENERATOR_CAPACITIES}, and all consumer demands in {POWER_DEMANDS} must be satisfied.\n\nIn short, we need a design and dispatch plan that respects physical limits and captures the true non\u2011linear nature of resistive losses. A model that only sums flows or assumes linear loss would understate heating costs and bias the solution. Our planning approach will evaluate candidate line builds based on distance, capacity, and copper conductor resistance, then route power to consumers to meet demand while honoring the square\u2011law cost of losses. This will give us a realistic balance between construction cost and operational efficiency, aligned with the data provided in {NODE_COORDINATES}, {NODE_TYPES}, {POWER_DEMANDS}, {GENERATOR_CAPACITIES}, {WIRE_RESISTIVITY}, {WIRE_CROSS_SECTION}, {OPERATING_VOLTAGE}, {LINE_CAPACITY}, {BUILD_COST_PER_KM}, and {LOSS_COST_RATE}."
